"""If the referee happens to have no card with him, the warning or the sending off may also be given without one. Penalties may be imposed until the match reports have been completed and signed. For offences after that, a report can be sent to the responsible commission.

Conduct in the face of unsporting behaviour: the referee has a duty to caution or warn players and support staff for unsporting behaviour and, in serious or repeated cases, to exclude them from the match. Such measures are to be taken only after particularly careful consideration. With every penalty, the consequences should be weighed: the yellow-and-red card has further effects during the match, the red card also after it.

What makes a good referee is the feel for which measure will calm a situation and is appropriate to it. Where a verbal caution does not seem sufficient, the referee has the right, depending on the severity of the offence, to impose: a warning (yellow card); a time penalty (yellow and red – exclusion for 10 points); a sending off (red card). A warning and a sending off may also be given to non-playing team members (coach, official, substitute).

Warning (yellow card) – enter the score, the player's name and the reason on the match report:
• Delaying the game: taking time returning the ball, or passing it through several players to the server. Warning against the team; for further delays a point for the opponent each time (time-wasting rule 6.4). Warning to the team captain if several players are involved. This yellow card does not count as player-related, so a further offence by the captain need not be followed by yellow-red.
• Kicking the ball away or hitting it high into the air.
• Abusing one's own team-mates, the opponents or the spectators.
• Loud emotional outbursts, including against oneself (swearing, kicking the post).
• Criticising the officials.
• Unsporting behaviour, for example wetting the ball with a wet shirt, mocking the opponent, verbal attacks, insinuations, walking back to one's position very slowly and possibly with the back to the opponent, repeatedly disturbing the service, arguments within the team.
• Deliberate unsporting behaviour, for instance in block situations, including disturbing by gestures.
• Incorrect conduct towards the officials: crowding the referee or linesman; getting worked up about decisions, especially when not the captain; an aggressive manner towards the referee, including as captain.
• Serving into the opponent's back.

Time penalty – yellow and red card: "If a player is excluded for a total of 10 points, the referee enters the time of the exclusion and the reason for it. A temporarily excluded player may not be replaced during that time. If a further player is sent off during that period, the match is to be abandoned." This time penalty can carry over into the next set.
• Insulting behaviour (abuse, physical attack on players, officials, spectators, the officiating team).
• Grossly unsporting behaviour (offensive criticism of the officials, threats, insults by word or gesture).
• Repetition of an offence already punished by a warning.

Sending off – red card:
• Dangerous play (negligently injuring an opponent or attempting to, including towards one's own team-mates).
• Insulting the officiating team (physical attack, gross abuse).
• Spitting – which may also lead to the match being abandoned.

Repeated offences: if a player has already been given a time penalty (yellow-red) earlier in the match, then after returning to the court he can no longer be given the same or a lesser penalty (yellow or yellow-red) for further offences. If he commits another offence that the referee wishes to punish with a card, it must be a sending off (red card).

Measures against spectators: spectators who disturb the match beyond reasonable limits – not by supporting their team from the stands, but for example by entering the court, throwing objects onto it, or discriminatory behaviour such as monkey chants at dark-skinned players – are dismissed by the referee and must leave the courtside or the stand; removing them is the organiser's task. The referee may suspend the match until the organiser has complied. If the organiser does not comply, the referee may abandon the match. The resulting penalty is decided by the organisation staging the match.""",
